Subject: [PATCH] Make mobile telemetry state observable instead of silent

STATUS: NOT APPLIED. Review, then from the repo root:
    git apply telemetry-observability.patch

Verified: applies cleanly to src/main.tsx at 63699db0, and `npx tsc --noEmit`
passes with it applied.

WHY THIS DOESN'T WAIT ON THE DISCRIMINATOR
------------------------------------------
Both orgs show zero Android events in 90 days, so DSN misrouting is ruled out —
events aren't being sent at all, and the PROD flag is the leading cause. But the
root problem isn't which flag is wrong. It's that "telemetry disabled" and
"telemetry enabled and quiet" are indistinguishable from outside the app. That's
what let this run unnoticed through a closed test, and it recurs in the React
Native app unless fixed structurally. So this is correct under either cause.

WHAT IT CHANGES
---------------
1. `enabled` no longer depends solely on import.meta.env.PROD — a native build
   always reports. package.json ships both `build` and `build:dev`, there's no
   Android CI, and the AAB is assembled by hand, so today one wrong script
   silently produces a blind release. Web dev is unchanged: local `vite` still
   sends nothing.

2. A startup log naming the resolved project id and enabled state, so
   `adb logcat | grep Telemetry` answers "is this build reporting?" in seconds.

3. A deliberate boot event, proving the pipeline end to end — init, egress from
   the WebView, correct project — rather than proving only that init() returned.
   It also makes real cold-start volume visible, which matters because the other
   explanation for zero events is that no tester has genuinely used the app, and
   that carries its own Play Store deadline.

TRADE-OFF NEEDING A DECISION
----------------------------
Item 3 sends one info event per cold start. Intentional while diagnosing at
closed-test volume; drop or sample it before a wide release, or it scales with
your install base. Written as one clearly-named call so it's easy to find.

NOTE
----
This touches src/, breaking the zero-diff discipline that protects Lovable's
ability to keep shipping the web app. That's why it's a patch for review and not
an applied change — and Lovable may overwrite it, so verify it survives.

